              Not everything in CMDS is a command              ===================================1010 of the 1036 files under /dd/CMDS are ordinary 68k program modules.The other 26 are not, and running one of them "to see if it works" provesnothing either way.  Here is what they are, as measured on 2026-09-19.`tools/module_census.py` in the source repository regenerates the wholelist by reading M$Type and M$Lang out of every header -- do that ratherthan trusting the figures above, which drift every time the disk grows.TRAP LIBRARIES -- type $0B.  A program links these; you do not run them.    cio         Microware's C library for cc 3.2 and earlier, and what the    math        star in DOC/INDEX marks.  math is software floating point;    math881     math881 uses a 68881 coprocessor.  Shipped by permission.    csl         Ultra C's C Shared Library, and the 68020/030/040 build of    csl020      it.  Shipped by permission.    X11R6shl    X11R6 shared library loader    graph       the Atari `Graph' library, in CMDS/GAMES.  g, striche,                apfel, sine, showpic, graphdemo and graphsave all link it.                It was catalogued as a graphics demo for years; it is a                library.    vmod_trap   in CMDS/COMMS: the trap handler rxmod and txmod call to                enter a module into the module directory.  `load' it                before running either of them.SYSTEM MODULES AND DRIVERS -- types $0C and $0E.  These belong in asystem's module directory, not on a command line.  Two are for any 68kmachine:    fpu               Microware's floating-point emulation module, for a                      machine with no 68881/68882.  It goes in your                      bootfile and your Init module's extension list.                      DOC/fpu.doc is its own document and its distribution                      grant, and the grant asks for that file to stay with                      it.    ptxm              Path Table eXtension Module: a kernel extension                      that lets user-state processes open unlimited I/O                      paths.  Courtesyware.  It installs into the kernel                      and so needs supervisor state; DOC/ptxm/ptxm.txt is                      its document.  (module name: Ptxm)and seven are MM/1 hardware:    keydrv.mm1        keyboard driver          (module name: keydrv)    msdrv.901_340     mouse driver             (module name: msdrv)    msdrv_340.901.ms  mouse driver, variant    (module name: msdrv)    snddrv            sound driver    rb37c65           floppy driver, 37C65 controller    windio.52         windowing terminal driver (module name: windio)    scsi_mm1a         SCSI subroutine moduleBASIC09 I-CODE -- type $02, language $02.  Not 68000 code at all: compiledBASIC09 I-code, which needs Microware's `runb` to run.  Nothing on this diskcan run them, and no amount of cio changes that.  They were once recorded hereas failing with an illegal instruction, which is true but beside the point.    bio         biorhythm chart (F. Kaefer 1987)    wysetime    Wyse terminal clock-setter    blackjack   Las Vegas blackjack (M. Theys) -- three modules in one file:                blackjack, draw and shuffle    dedit       disk sector editor (Carl Kreider) -- NINE modules in the one                file: dedit, hex_dec, dec_hex, get_media_size, p_sector,                go_xy, p_sir, e_sector, rd_sectorThe way to tell one of these from a broken 68000 program is to start it:OS-9 answers E_BMID (205), `bad module ID', because it was asked to forkI-code as machine code.  That message reads like damage and is not.  It ishow `dedit' came to be excluded from this collection once, and how `bio'nearly was.  Compare the header bytes against `bio' before believing aprogram is broken: an I-code module has $00 where a 68000 program has $01.DATA MODULES -- type $04.    rtfdat      RTF FORTRAN data module    wn.stb      WN's symbol tableNOT MODULES AT ALL -- three scripts.    man         formats and pages a manual page -- a ksh script that runs                nroff -man    who         who is logged in -- Microware shell syntax    mscheck     mtools disk verifier -- a ksh script(`keep', `kept' and `unkeep' were scripts here once and are compiledmodules now.  See DOC/README-KEEP.)MODULE GROUPS -- one file, several modules.  OS-9 loads the whole group andkeeps it resident until the combined link count reaches zero, so the extramodules are not leftovers.    lfmaker     lfmaker (program) + lfmaker_win (window descriptor)    cyberwar    CyberWar and cyber_game (both programs) + cyber_start_win                and cyber_retry_win (window descriptors)    blackjack   blackjack, draw and shuffle (all BASIC09 I-code)    dedit       eleven, all I-code: dedit, hex_dec, dec_hex,                get_media_size, p_sector, go_xy, p_sir, e_sector,                rd_sector, p_fd and map_sectorA few other files are a byte or two longer than their module -- trailingpadding, not a second module.  Count $4AFC headers, not bytes, beforecalling anything a group.ONE MORE THING WORTH KNOWING38 files report a module name that is not their filename, as measured on2026-09-19.  Nearly all of it is the original author's own capitalisation-- `aterm' answers to ATerm, `atob' to AtoB, `lha' to LHa, `ckermit' towermit -- or a deliberate alternate: `math881' answers to `math' and`csl020' to `csl' precisely so that a program linking the plain namefinds the right build on its CPU.NONE OF THEM IS OURS ANY MORE, and that is a correction.  This file usedto name seven as a build mistake of this collection's own -- a missing`-n=<name>` at link time, so the module took its name from the sourcefile of the moment.  Five of them really were: `compress' answered toR_compress, `kermit' to R_kermit, `screen' to R_screen, `queens' toB_baruch (its source is baruch.c, a 1990 IOCCC entry) and `wish' toB_wish.  All five have been rebuilt with their own names and each wasread back out of its header to check.`tabs' and `hc' still answer to B_tabs and B_hc, and they were neverrebuilt here -- neither has a recipe.  Those names came with them fromtheir archives, EFFO forum disk 6 and a Usenet misc.ar.  They run; theyjust report a name of their author's choosing in their own usage textand in a module directory listing.